{"entities": [{"id": "arc42/arc42", "type": "arc42", "slug": "arc42", "path": "knowledge/arc42.md", "rev": "2730a468c51c", "lenses": [{"code": "direction", "name": "Which way the arrows point", "section": "Building Block View", "instruction": "Read the diagram as a stranger would: can they tell what depends on what, and what\nwould break in which direction? A box-and-line picture with no direction is a\nseating chart. Check it against the `depends_on` edges \u2014 where the prose and the\ngraph disagree, one of them is out of date.", "q": "1bab232ee74d", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "deferred", "name": "Deferred, with a reason it can wait", "section": "Deferred Decisions", "instruction": "Each deferred item should say what would force it back onto the table. \"Later\"\nwithout a trigger is how a spine quietly rots \u2014 nobody ever notices the moment it\nstopped being safe to wait.", "q": "ab7ad3cf3dad", "state": "unreviewed"}]}, {"id": "prd/prd", "type": "prd", "slug": "prd", "path": "knowledge/prd.md", "rev": "e3116b43b6f5", "lenses": [{"code": "scope", "name": "The line, from both sides", "section": null, "instruction": "Non-Goals is where a PRD earns its keep, and it is the section that decays first.\nDoes it name the thing a reasonable reader would assume is in scope? \"Not this\nproduct, ever\" and \"not this version, and here is what would change that\" are\ndifferent claims \u2014 the second belongs under Deferred.", "q": "005a15e40d00", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "journeys", "name": "Journeys reach capabilities", "section": null, "instruction": "Every journey should have capabilities that make it possible, and every capability\nshould serve a journey. A capability no journey needs is either load-bearing for a\nreason nobody wrote down, or it is not needed. A journey that only ever ends well\ncannot exercise the failure handling the product also has to do.", "q": "c2df720d6e28", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "metrics", "name": "Measurable, dated, and guarded", "section": null, "instruction": "A success metric needs a number, a date and a source of truth. Then ask the harder\nquestion: what could someone break while moving it? That answer is the\ncounter-metric, and it is as load-bearing as the target.", "q": "fec035278aeb", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "assumptions", "name": "Assumptions, marked as such", "section": null, "instruction": "Scan for claims about users, volumes or the market that are stated as fact. Mark\neach one inline as `[ASSUMPTION: \u2026]` so a later reader can tell what was known\nfrom what was guessed \u2014 the two look identical in prose six months on.", "q": "6e50f358b2ff", "state": "unreviewed"}]}, {"id": "requirement/req-invoice-totals", "type": "requirement", "slug": "req-invoice-totals", "path": "knowledge/requirements/req-invoice-totals.md", "rev": "15dfd18b9df0", "lenses": [{"code": "single", "name": "One statement", "section": null, "instruction": "Does the body state exactly one thing? An \"and\", a bulleted list of behaviours, or\ntwo sentences each with their own verb mean this is two requirements. Split it \u2014\ntwo records each with their own `realized_in` are worth more than one that cannot\nbe satisfied or violated as a unit.", "q": "651318ff2db7", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "testable", "name": "Observable from outside", "section": null, "instruction": "Could someone who did not write this tell whether the system satisfies it, without\nreading the implementation? If the only way to check is to open the code, this is\ndescribing a design, not stating a requirement.", "q": "00464b128ef4", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "measurable", "name": "A number and its measurement", "section": null, "instruction": "Look for the value, the unit, where it is measured, over what window, and what\nenforces it. Flag \"gracefully\", \"robust\", \"scalable\", \"efficient\", \"fast\",\n\"user-friendly\" \u2014 an adjective is not a bound. \"p99 under 200 ms at the API edge,\nmeasured over a rolling hour, alerted on in the latency dashboard\" is.", "q": "7795032b7267", "state": "unreviewed"}, {"code": "realized", "name": "Where it is satisfied", "section": null, "instruction": "`realized_in` is the only edge tying a rule to the code that satisfies it, and a\nrequirement without one is unfinished by design. If no component satisfies it yet,\nthat is the honest answer and the gap is the point \u2014 but check that it is not\nsimply unlinked. If a use case depends on this rule, name it in `use_cases`: a\nrule no flow needs is either global or unowned, and the graph should say which.", "q": "7d7a54c8b5a0", "state": "unreviewed"}]}], "skipped": []}
